### How do min/max statistics work?

A Parquet file is split into row groups, each row group into column chunks, and each column chunk into pages. At both levels, Parquet stores the minimum and maximum value of each column. When Spark pushes a predicate, the reader compares it to each block's range and skips any block that cannot match.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class MinMaxStatistics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Filter: amount > 100000");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row Group 0: min=500, max=50000");
        System.out.println("  50000 < 100000 -> SKIP (no decompress)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row Group 1: min=45000, max=200000");
        System.out.println("  200000 >= 100000 -> READ");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row Group 2: min=100, max=30000");
        System.out.println("  30000 < 100000 -> SKIP");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Result: read 1 of 3 row groups.");
        System.out.println("Saved: 67% I/O skipped.");
    }
}
```

```text
Filter: amount > 100000

Row Group 0: min=500, max=50000
  50000 < 100000 -> SKIP (no decompress)

Row Group 1: min=45000, max=200000
  200000 >= 100000 -> READ

Row Group 2: min=100, max=30000
  30000 < 100000 -> SKIP

Result: read 1 of 3 row groups.
Saved: 67% I/O skipped.
```

### How does dictionary filtering work?

Parquet often dictionary-encodes a column, storing distinct values once in a dictionary page. For an equality filter like `txn_type = 'wire'`, the reader checks the dictionary page: if 'wire' is not in the dictionary, no row can match, and the block is skipped.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class DictionaryFiltering {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Block's dictionary page for txn_type:");
        System.out.println("  {0: 'card', 1: 'upi', 2: 'neft'}");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Filter: txn_type = 'wire'");
        System.out.println("  'wire' NOT in dictionary -> SKIP block");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Filter: txn_type = 'upi'");
        System.out.println("  'upi' IN dictionary -> READ block");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Effective for low-to-medium cardinality.");
    }
}
```

```text
Block's dictionary page for txn_type:
  {0: 'card', 1: 'upi', 2: 'neft'}

Filter: txn_type = 'wire'
  'wire' NOT in dictionary -> SKIP block

Filter: txn_type = 'upi'
  'upi' IN dictionary -> READ block

Effective for low-to-medium cardinality.
```

### How do bloom filters work?

A bloom filter is a compact bitset where each value is hashed into several bit positions. To test: hash the value and check those bits. If any bit is zero, the value is definitely not present and the block is skipped. If all bits are set, the value is possibly present and the block is read. No false negatives, only false positives.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class BloomFilterExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bloom filter for txn_id column:");
        System.out.println("  Bitset: [0,1,1,0,1,0,0,1,...]");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Test txn_id = 'T99999':");
        System.out.println("  hash1('T99999') -> bit 3 = 0");
        System.out.println("  Definitely NOT present -> SKIP block");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Test txn_id = 'T10045':");
        System.out.println("  hash1 -> bit 1 = 1");
        System.out.println("  hash2 -> bit 4 = 1");
        System.out.println("  hash3 -> bit 7 = 1");
        System.out.println("  Possibly present -> READ block");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Key: no false negatives, safe for skipping.");
    }
}
```

```text
Bloom filter for txn_id column:
  Bitset: [0,1,1,0,1,0,0,1,...]

Test txn_id = 'T99999':
  hash1('T99999') -> bit 3 = 0
  Definitely NOT present -> SKIP block

Test txn_id = 'T10045':
  hash1 -> bit 1 = 1
  hash2 -> bit 4 = 1
  hash3 -> bit 7 = 1
  Possibly present -> READ block

Key: no false negatives, safe for skipping.
```

### Why is sort order critical for min/max?

Min/max statistics only help if the per-block ranges are tight and non-overlapping. If data is sorted by the filter column, each block holds a narrow slice. If unsorted, every block spans the whole domain and no block is ever skipped.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class SortOrderMatters {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("SORTED by amount:");
        System.out.println("  Block 0: [0, 10000]");
        System.out.println("  Block 1: [10001, 20000]");
        System.out.println("  Block 2: [20001, 50000]");
        System.out.println("  Filter: amount > 30000");
        System.out.println("  -> Blocks 0,1 SKIPPED. Block 2 READ.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("UNSORTED:");
        System.out.println("  Block 0: [50, 49000]");
        System.out.println("  Block 1: [100, 48000]");
        System.out.println("  Block 2: [75, 50000]");
        System.out.println("  Filter: amount > 30000");
        System.out.println("  -> ALL blocks read. Stats useless.");
    }
}
```

```text
SORTED by amount:
  Block 0: [0, 10000]
  Block 1: [10001, 20000]
  Block 2: [20001, 50000]
  Filter: amount > 30000
  -> Blocks 0,1 SKIPPED. Block 2 READ.

UNSORTED:
  Block 0: [50, 49000]
  Block 1: [100, 48000]
  Block 2: [75, 50000]
  Filter: amount > 30000
  -> ALL blocks read. Stats useless.
```

### How do the three mechanisms compare?

| Mechanism | Type | Predicate | Cardinality | Default | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Min/max | Exact range | Range (>, <, =) | Any | On | None (in footer) |
| Dictionary | Exact membership | Equality (=) | Low-medium | On (if encoded) | None |
| Bloom filter | Probabilistic | Equality (=) | High | Off (opt-in) | Space at write |

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ThreeWayComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("When each mechanism shines:");
        System.out.println("  Min/max: range filters on sorted columns");
        System.out.println("  Dictionary: equality on low-card columns");
        System.out.println("  Bloom: equality on high-card columns");
    }
}
```

```text
When each mechanism shines:
  Min/max: range filters on sorted columns
  Dictionary: equality on low-card columns
  Bloom: equality on high-card columns
```

### How do you enable bloom filters?

Bloom filters must be explicitly enabled per column at write time because they cost space. You enable them only for high-cardinality columns you filter by equality.

```python
# Enable bloom filter on txn_id at write time
df.write.format("parquet") \
    .option("parquet.bloom.filter.enabled#txn_id", "true") \
    .option("parquet.bloom.filter.expected.ndv#txn_id", "1000000") \
    .save("s3://bank-lake/curated/transactions/")
```

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class EnableBloomFilter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bloom filter config:");
        System.out.println("  Enabled per column at write time.");
        System.out.println("  Costs space (extra bytes per block).");
        System.out.println("  Enable only for high-cardinality"
            + " equality-filtered columns.");
        System.out.println("  Example: txn_id, account_id");
        System.out.println("  Not needed for: branch (use dictionary)");
    }
}
```

```text
Bloom filter config:
  Enabled per column at write time.
  Costs space (extra bytes per block).
  Enable only for high-cardinality equality-filtered columns.
  Example: txn_id, account_id
  Not needed for: branch (use dictionary)
```

### How do you verify pushdown in Spark?

Check the physical plan for PushedFilters to verify that predicates are being pushed to the Parquet reader.

```python
df = spark.read.parquet("s3://bank-lake/curated/transactions/")
result = df.filter("amount > 100000").select("txn_id", "amount")
result.explain(True)
```

```text
== Physical Plan ==
*(1) Filter (amount > 100000)
+- FileScan parquet [txn_id, amount]
   PushedFilters: [GreaterThan(amount, 100000)]
   ReadSchema: struct<txn_id:string, amount:decimal(15,2)>
```

